---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import CompressPdf from '../../components/tools/CompressPdf.tsx';
import RelatedTools from '../../components/layout/RelatedTools';
import { relatedTools } from '../../data/related-tools';
---

<ToolLayout
  title="Compress PDF Free Online — Reduce PDF File Size | Filekrush"
  description="Compress PDF files to reduce file size. Works best on scanned documents and image-heavy PDFs. No upload, runs in your browser."
  canonical="/compress-pdf"
  heading="Compress PDF"
  subheading="Reduce PDF file size for email, uploads, and forms — free, private, instant."
>
  <CompressPdf client:only="react" />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <section class="prose prose-gray max-w-none">
      <div class="toc">
        <p>On this page</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="#compression-levels">Which compression level do I need?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#how-it-works">How it works</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-gets-compressed">What gets compressed</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2 id="compression-levels">Which compression level do I need?</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Level</th>
            <th>Best for</th>
            <th>Trade-off</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Maximum</td>
            <td><a href="/compress-pdf/for-email/">Email attachments</a>, portals with strict upload limits</td>
            <td>Smallest possible file; scanned text and images may look visibly softer.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Balanced</td>
            <td>Most uploads — resumes, applications, general sharing</td>
            <td>Noticeable size reduction with quality that still looks clean.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Light</td>
            <td>Archival copies, documents where clarity matters most</td>
            <td>Smallest size reduction, but preserves quality closest to the original.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>Sending a scanned document by email?</strong> Scanned PDFs are often 5-20MB because each page is stored as a high-resolution image. Maximum compression on a scanned document routinely cuts it by 80-90% with barely visible quality loss — see the <a href="/compress-pdf/for-email/">compress PDF for email</a> guide.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Drop your PDF</strong> — supports any PDF file up to 50MB.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose compression level</strong> — Maximum for smallest size, Balanced for most uses, Light for best quality.</li>
        <li><strong>Download</strong> — your compressed PDF is ready instantly.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="what-gets-compressed">What gets compressed</h2>
      <p>This tool re-encodes the images embedded inside your PDF at lower quality and smaller dimensions. It works best on scanned documents, photo-heavy PDFs, and documents with large embedded images. Text-only PDFs — reports, contracts, forms typed directly into a document — may see little to no size reduction, since there's no image data to compress.</p>

      <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>

      <h3>Why didn't my PDF get smaller?</h3>
      <p>If your PDF is mostly typed text with no embedded images, there's very little to compress — text itself takes up almost no space. Compression makes the biggest difference on scanned pages, photos, or PDFs exported with high-resolution images.</p>

      <h3>Will compression make scanned text unreadable?</h3>
      <p>At Balanced or Light settings, no — text stays legible. At Maximum, very fine print can get slightly softer. If your document is text-heavy and you need to preserve maximum clarity, use Light compression instead.</p>

      <h3>Can I compress a password-protected PDF?</h3>
      <p>No — Filekrush needs to read the PDF's content to compress it, which isn't possible while it's encrypted. Remove the password first using a PDF editor, then compress.</p>

      <h3>Is there a size limit?</h3>
      <p>Filekrush handles PDFs up to 50MB. Since everything runs in your browser, very large files may just take longer to process depending on your device.</p>

      <h3>Does compressing change the number of pages or the text content?</h3>
      <p>No. Compression only affects how embedded images are encoded — page count, text, and layout stay exactly the same.</p>
    </section>

    <RelatedTools title="PDF compression options" tools={relatedTools.compressPdf} />
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
